.nh
.TH fttyd 1 "" fttyd "User Manual"

.SH NAME
.PP
fttyd \- Share your terminal over the web, with a file manager and stats sidebar


.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\fBfttyd\fP [options] <command> [<arguments...>]


.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
fttyd is a drop-in replacement for ttyd(1): it accepts the same options, serves the same
endpoints and speaks the same terminal protocol, and adds a sidebar with live system
statistics and a file tree that can download, upload and edit files.

.PP
The terminal is one PTY per websocket connection. Each connection starts the configured
command, receives the PTY output as it is produced, and is closed when the child exits.

.PP
Read-only is the default. Without \fB\-\-writable\fP neither the terminal nor the file
API accepts input.


.SH OPTIONS
.PP
-p, --port
      Port to listen (default: 7681, use \fB0\fP for a random port)

.PP
-i, --interface
      Network interface or address to bind (eg: eth0, 127.0.0.1), or a UNIX domain
      socket path when it ends in .sock or .socket (eg: /var/run/fttyd.sock)

.PP
-U, --socket-owner
      Owner of the UNIX domain socket file (eg: user:group)

.PP
-c, --credential USER:PASSWORD
      Credential for HTTP Basic authentication

.PP
-H, --auth-header
      Header an authenticating reverse proxy sets; when present the request is
      authenticated and the header value is logged as the actor of file operations

.PP
-u, --uid
      User id to run with, applied after the listener is bound

.PP
-g, --gid
      Group id to run with, applied after the listener is bound

.PP
-s, --signal
      Signal sent to the command on exit (default: 1, SIGHUP)

.PP
-w, --cwd
      Working directory for the child program

.PP
-a, --url-arg
      Allow the client to append arguments in the URL (eg: ?arg=foo&arg=bar)

.PP
-W, --writable
      Allow clients to write to the terminal and to the file API

.PP
-t, --client-option KEY=VALUE
      Option passed to the browser client; repeat for more options. The value is parsed
      as JSON when possible, otherwise sent as a string

.PP
-T, --terminal-type
      Terminal type to report (default: xterm-256color)

.PP
-O, --check-origin
      Refuse websocket connections from a different origin

.PP
-m, --max-clients
      Maximum number of clients (default: 0, no limit)

.PP
-o, --once
      Accept only one client and exit when it disconnects

.PP
-q, --exit-no-conn
      Exit when the last client disconnects

.PP
-B, --browser
      Open the terminal in the default browser

.PP
-I, --index
      Custom index.html to serve instead of the built-in interface

.PP
-b, --base-path
      Base path expected from a reverse proxy (eg: /mounted/here)

.PP
-P, --ping-interval
      Websocket ping interval in seconds (default: 5)

.PP
-f, --srv-buf-size
      Maximum chunk size read from the PTY (default: 4096)

.PP
-6, --ipv6
      Listen on IPv6

.PP
-S, --ssl
      Enable TLS; requires --ssl-cert and --ssl-key

.PP
-C, --ssl-cert
      TLS certificate chain (PEM)

.PP
-K, --ssl-key
      TLS private key (PEM)

.PP
-A, --ssl-ca
      CA bundle used to require and verify a client certificate

.PP
-d, --debug
      Log level (default: 7)

.PP
--file-root
      Restrict the file API to this directory; repeatable. Without it the file API can
      reach everything the server process can, which is the same reach as the terminal

.PP
-v, --version
      Print the version and exit

.PP
-h, --help
      Print the usage text and exit


.SH ENDPOINTS
.PP
\fB/ws\fP is the terminal websocket and \fB/token\fP returns the credential the client
uses for it. The sidebar uses \fB/api/info\fP, \fB/api/fs\fP, \fB/api/download\fP,
\fB/api/archive\fP, \fB/api/stats\fP and \fB/api/upload\fP. All paths honour \fB-b\fP.

.PP
Uploads and mutations require \fB-W\fP and the CSRF header named by \fB/api/info\fP.


.SH FILES
.PP
No files are required at runtime: the interface is embedded in the binary.


.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
0 on a clean shutdown, 1 for configuration errors, 255 for invalid options.


.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
ttyd(1)
